GBA SLIP networking - host side
===============================

The GBA (GBAED build) runs a small IP host, /usr/sbin/slipd, on its
link-cable UART through /dev/sio.  These two scripts connect it to a
Linux IP interface when the link cable is on a Windows PC and Linux runs
in WSL2 (whose kernel has no SLIP driver):

    GBA --link cable/USB serial (COM3)-- com_bridge.ps1 (Windows)
        --TCP localhost:5555-- slip_tun.py (WSL2) --TUN gba0-- Linux IP

  com_bridge.ps1  copies bytes between the serial port and TCP.  Windows
                  PowerShell 5.1 and .NET only.  RTS/CTS handshaking on.
  slip_tun.py     SLIP-frames IPv4 packets between the TCP connection and
                  a TUN interface.  --fake answers pings itself (no GBA).

One-time setup (as root, in WSL2):

    ip tuntap add dev gba0 mode tun user $USER
    ip addr add 192.168.7.1 peer 192.168.7.2 dev gba0
    ip link set gba0 up

FTDI cables: Device Manager -> the COM port -> Port Settings -> Advanced
-> Latency Timer 1 ms (default 16) shortens round trips.

Running:

 1. On the GBA (serial console or LCD):   slipd &
    It takes the UART off the serial console while it runs and sets the
    line to 38400 (-b to change).  `slipd -t 60` exits after 60 s
    without traffic, which gives the serial console back.
 2. In WSL2:      python3 slip_tun.py
 3. In Windows:   powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File com_bridge.ps1
    (from a copy on a Windows drive: scripts on \\wsl.localhost do not
    run).  -Baud must match slipd's rate (default 38400 for both); the
    serial console itself is 115200.
 4. ping 192.168.7.2
 5. TCP services:  nc 192.168.7.2 7 (echo), 9 (discard), 19 (chargen)

Testing slipd without a GBA: `slip_tun.py --pty` runs SLIP on a pty
instead of TCP and prints its name; `make slipd-host` in usr.sbin/slipd
builds slipd for Linux, run as `./slipd-host -d /dev/pts/N`.  --drop P
throws away P% of the packets each way, to exercise TCP's retransmission.

Line rate: the GBA UART has a 4-byte receive FIFO and no "byte arrived"
interrupt, so bytes coming to the GBA are only safe while it polls.
slip_tun.py puts extra END bytes before each packet (--lead, default 12),
so bytes lost while slipd wakes up fall on padding.  Measured with TCP:
38400 gives about 2.3 KB/s to the GBA and 2.8 KB/s from it; 115200 with
--lead 40 (12 is too short there) about 5.4 and 6.5 KB/s, no retransmits
over 100 KB.
